    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$\\cos^2 2x-\\sin^2 2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=15^\\circ,75^\\circ,135^\\circ,195^\\circ,255^\\circ,315^\\circ$.\n\nUse double-angle identities to obtain a quadratic in $\\sin2x$.",
          "worked": "Use $\\cos^2 2x-\\sin^2 2x=\\cos4x$ and $2\\sin x\\cos x=\\sin2x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos4x&=\\sin2x\\\\1-2\\sin^2 2x&=\\sin2x\\\\2\\sin^2 2x+\\sin2x-1&=0\\\\(2\\sin2x-1)(\\sin2x+1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\sin2x=\\frac12$ or $\\sin2x=-1$. For $0^\\circ\\le2x\\le720^\\circ$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2x&=30^\\circ,150^\\circ,270^\\circ,390^\\circ,510^\\circ,630^\\circ\\\\x&=15^\\circ,75^\\circ,135^\\circ,195^\\circ,255^\\circ,315^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 78
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$\\cos2x-3\\cos x+2=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=0^\\circ,60^\\circ,300^\\circ,360^\\circ$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2\\cos^2x-1-3\\cos x+2&=0\\\\2\\cos^2x-3\\cos x+1&=0\\\\(2\\cos x-1)(\\cos x-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\cos x=\\frac12$ or $\\cos x=1$.\n\n$$x=0^\\circ,60^\\circ,300^\\circ,360^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$2\\sin^2x+\\sin^2 2x=2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=45^\\circ,90^\\circ,135^\\circ,225^\\circ,270^\\circ,315^\\circ$.\n\nExpand $\\sin2x$ and factor in $\\sin^2x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}2\\sin^2x+4\\sin^2x\\cos^2x&=2\\\\2\\sin^2x+4\\sin^2x(1-\\sin^2x)&=2\\\\4\\sin^4x-6\\sin^2x+2&=0\\\\2(2\\sin^2x-1)(\\sin^2x-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\sin^2x=\\frac12$ or $\\sin^2x=1$, so $\\sin x=\\pm\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}$ or $\\sin x=\\pm1$.\n\n$$x=45^\\circ,90^\\circ,135^\\circ,225^\\circ,270^\\circ,315^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$(1-\\tan x)(1+\\sin2x)=1+\\tan x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=0^\\circ,135^\\circ,180^\\circ,315^\\circ,360^\\circ$.\n\nMultiply by $\\cos x$ and factor without cancelling $\\sin x$.",
          "worked": "The original equation requires $\\cos x\\ne0$. Substitute $\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ and $\\sin2x=2\\sin x\\cos x$, then multiply by $\\cos x$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\cos x-\\sin x)(1+2\\sin x\\cos x)&=\\cos x+\\sin x\\\\2\\sin x\\cos^2x-2\\sin^2x\\cos x-2\\sin x&=0\\\\2\\sin x(\\cos^2x-\\sin x\\cos x-1)&=0\\\\-2\\sin^2x(\\sin x+\\cos x)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence $\\sin x=0$ or $\\sin x+\\cos x=0$. These give $x=0^\\circ,180^\\circ,360^\\circ$ or $\\tan x=-1$, respectively. All satisfy $\\cos x\\ne0$.\n\n$$x=0^\\circ,135^\\circ,180^\\circ,315^\\circ,360^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 94
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$\\dfrac{\\sin2x}{\\sin x}=\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\cos2x}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=30^\\circ,90^\\circ,150^\\circ,210^\\circ,270^\\circ,330^\\circ$.\n\nRetain the $\\cos x=0$ branch and check the original denominators.",
          "worked": "The original equation requires $\\sin x\\ne0$ and $\\cos2x\\ne0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2\\cos x&=\\frac{\\cos x}{\\cos2x}\\\\2\\cos x\\cos2x&=\\cos x\\\\\\cos x(2\\cos2x-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\cos x=0$ or $\\cos2x=\\frac12$. The first gives $x=90^\\circ,270^\\circ$. For the second,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2x&=60^\\circ,300^\\circ,420^\\circ,660^\\circ\\\\x&=30^\\circ,150^\\circ,210^\\circ,330^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\n\nEvery candidate has $\\sin x\\ne0$ and $\\cos2x\\ne0$.\n\n$$x=30^\\circ,90^\\circ,150^\\circ,210^\\circ,270^\\circ,330^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 94
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "$\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x=\\frac12\\sin2x$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=45^\\circ,225^\\circ$.\n\nUse $\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x=1-\\frac12\\sin^2 2x$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x&=(\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x)^2-2\\sin^2x\\cos^2x\\\\\\sin^4x+\\cos^4x&=1-\\frac12\\sin^2 2x\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitute into the equation.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1-\\frac12\\sin^2 2x&=\\frac12\\sin2x\\\\\\sin^2 2x+\\sin2x-2&=0\\\\(\\sin2x+2)(\\sin2x-1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe value $\\sin2x=-2$ is impossible. Thus $\\sin2x=1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2x&=90^\\circ,450^\\circ\\\\x&=45^\\circ,225^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 86
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "$\\cos2x\\cos x-\\sin2x\\sin x=\\frac12$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=20^\\circ,100^\\circ,140^\\circ,220^\\circ,260^\\circ,340^\\circ$.",
          "worked": "Use the cosine sum formula in reverse.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos(2x+x)&=\\frac12\\\\\\cos3x&=\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $0^\\circ\\le3x\\le1080^\\circ$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3x&=60^\\circ,300^\\circ,420^\\circ,660^\\circ,780^\\circ,1020^\\circ\\\\x&=20^\\circ,100^\\circ,140^\\circ,220^\\circ,260^\\circ,340^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p48-q25-32-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "$\\tan x+\\tan x\\tan2x+\\tan2x=1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=15^\\circ,75^\\circ,195^\\circ,255^\\circ$.\n\nUse tangent addition; exclude $135^\\circ$ and $315^\\circ$ because $\\tan2x$ is undefined.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\tan x+\\tan2x&=1-\\tan x\\tan2x\\\\\\frac{\\tan x+\\tan2x}{1-\\tan x\\tan2x}&=1\\\\\\tan3x&=1\\end{align*}$$\n\nDivision introduces no lost solution: if $\\tan x\\tan2x=1$, the preceding equation would also require $\\tan x+\\tan2x=0$, which is impossible for real ratios with product 1.\n\nFor $0^\\circ\\le3x\\le1080^\\circ$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}3x&=45^\\circ,225^\\circ,405^\\circ,585^\\circ,765^\\circ,945^\\circ\\\\x&=15^\\circ,75^\\circ,135^\\circ,195^\\circ,255^\\circ,315^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\n\nAt $135^\\circ$ and $315^\\circ$, $\\tan2x$ is undefined. The remaining candidates satisfy the original equation.\n\n$$x=15^\\circ,75^\\circ,195^\\circ,255^\\circ.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 124
      }
    ]
  },
